  {
    "sha": "35020f6cc294c3b6266be720b3c9d2b178c970f9",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKDM1MDIwZjZjYzI5NGMzYjYyNjZiZTcyMGIzYzlkMmIxNzhjOTcwZjk",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-21T09:19:24Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-21T09:19:24Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(slot): split the wall lap at each row end so no row join crosses it\n\nThe verifier's crossing law flattens an arc into chords inside its circle. A row join ends exactly on the circle, so it crossed the flattened quarter arcs of the lap, and the post of each wide slot was held (8 app suites). Each cap is now split at every row end and at its tip. The rows and the lap compute a row end with one function (cap_point), so they share each vertex to the bit, and the law reads a touch.\n\nTests: slot_round_ends checks the crossing law and a slot whose rows are off the cap tips. Expected-change updates: operations and example_catalog count only descending arcs as an orbit, or the lap arcs; clearance_in_stock: the slot's traverse between levels now rides over its own channel and is acquitted, and the post stays held by the first traverse and the rapid into the stock.\n\nRefs #2906\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
